WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:07.380
Tonight I ordered a targeted military strike
on the airfield in Syria

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.220
from where the chemical attack was launched.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:16.420
Hi, I'm Nick Gillespie for Reason, we're talking
with Emma Ashford of the CATO Institute.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.260
She holds a PHD in foreign affairs from University
of Virginia

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.980
and is an expert on the Middle East.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.160
Emma, thanks for talking to us.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.320
Good to be here.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.320
President Donald Trump ordered a missile strike
against a Syrian airbase.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.420
What's the likely outcome of this action with
regard to the Syrian civil war

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.660
and to US relations with allies in the area.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.980
To some extent it depends on where this goes
next.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.980
This strike appears to be something of a one-off,
that Trump is portraying it as, you know,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.520
'we are hitting the Syrians hard, a punitive
action

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.520
to show them they can't use chemical weapons.'

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.820
Which was the proximate cause for the strike.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.080
Exactly that's, at least that's what the Trump
administration is saying is that they-

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.720
they're carrying this out because they believe preventing
chemical weapons use

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.740
is in the US national interest.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.700
Allies in the area, in the region, have claimed
that this is a welcome development.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.440
But what they mean by that is that they also
want the Trump administration to start taking

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.460
a harder line on the Assad regime.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.700
And they would like to see Trump take further
action against Assad.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.859
So who're the allies in the area that are
pushing for that?

00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:25.600
Because, obviously Russia, we assume, not
quite an ally, but in the civil war they are.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.540
Who's pushing for a harder line or more engagement?

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.820
Well the Russians are actually pretty unhappy
about this development

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.840
because the Russians thought that the Trump administration

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.820
would be more open to a negotiated settlement.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.140
The people in the region who are happy are
the Saudis, and the Qataris,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.459
the United Arab Emirates, to a lesser extent Turkey.

00:01:44.459 --> 00:01:49.069
These countries that have been pushing to
get Assad out for the last five-six years

00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:51.549
and they've been wanting US support that whole
time.

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:54.140
The Obama administration didn't give it to
them.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.960
The question now is whether the strike is
a one-off

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.660
or whether Trump is actually going to follow through on that.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:06.560
It is in this vital national security interest
of the United States

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:14.060
to prevent and deter the spread and use of deadly chemical weapons.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.700
Is he right about that?

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.140
That's a difficult question, right?

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.680
I mean we think we all benefit from the norm

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.380
and indeed the international law against chemical weapons use.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.060
But they're not actually particularly good
weapons of war.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:32.050
And if you look at the Syrian situation, the
Assad regime killed less than a hundred people

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:34.620
in this chemical weapons attack the other
day.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.520
They've killed thousands, hundreds of thousands
over the last six years,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.900
and yet we don't appear to find that to be a national security interest or problem for us.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:51.180
So it's really, again, not clear whether Trump
thinks this is a humanitarian intervention

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.380
or whether he thinks that it's a security
intervention.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.200
Does he think that we are threatened by Syria's
chemical weapons?

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.500
Should the US be doing humanitarian interventions
in Syria? as opposed to-

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.120
and that's leaving aside whether or not it is a security one, but-

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.620
Absolutely not.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.540
And I think it's really instructive here to
compare Obama and Trump

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.840
because they've faced exactly the same situation.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.200
The Assad regime attacking civilians with
sarin gas

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.460
and they responded in very different ways.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.260
So Obama is often criticized for that red
line that he made,

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.100
it was a really stupid statement,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.180
but he then went on and forged
this really successful diplomatic compromise,

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.520
brought in the Russians, they removed the
vast majority of the chemical weapons

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.580
and you noticed that for the time that Obama was
in office there was no chemical weapons attack.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.780
It's only now that we have a new president

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.620
that the Syrians have decided to test this norm again.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:48.400
And I don't think a lot of people were satisfied
with Obama's stance toward Syria

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.400
because it was- he set the red line and he was pushing for it

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.980
and he got pulled back by Rand Paul and Justin Amash

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.300
and a couple of other members of Congress.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:01.000
But it's clear also that going boots in is
not a good idea.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.730
What is the, you know, and more broadly in
the middle east, we invaded Iraq in 2003,

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:11.740
we pulled out in 2011 our combat troops, we're
still involved in the area.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:16.140
We're now in the third president, in the 21st
century they're three for three

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.100
of getting involved in the middle east.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.740
What is the essential lesson that presidents
and both parties

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:25.740
as well as Democrats and Republicans need to learn from,

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.580
you know, it's a quagmire, I mean, we're stuck in the middle east.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.880
What is the essential lesson that we need
to learn that we have not yet mastered?

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.480
You know, if we go back to that Obama decision
not to intervene in Syria in 2013,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.200
I think it's pretty clear that he had learned the
lessons of Iraq

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.760
and particularly of his own failed intervention in Libya.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:49.180
He looked at both of those and he saw that while
the initial military action might go well,

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.460
we end up creating more of a problem than
we actually solve.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.479
And there's actually good academic evidence
that probably more people died in Libya

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:01.050
as a result of the US intervention than if we
just had not intervened in the first place.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.800
So whenever we intervene in these conflicts
we seem to make things worse

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.300
and when Obama made his choice on chemical weapons,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.700
he said
'well a diplomatic compromise might be imperfect,

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.889
but it's far better than military strikes
that will lead who knows where.'

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:18.920
Trump doesn't seem to have that level of forethought,
he's made the military strikes

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.280
and who knows what the next step is?

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.160
Well we will leave it there. Thank you.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:28.400
We've been talking with Emma Ashford at the
CATO Institute, for Reason, I'm Nick Gillespie.

